/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/google-cdl.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("google-cdl", [{"t":"What cloud computing is and why it drives digital transformation","body":["Cloud computing is the on-demand delivery of computing resources, such as servers, storage, databases, networking, analytics and AI, over the internet, with pay-as-you-go pricing. Instead of buying and running hardware in your own building, you rent capacity from a provider such as Google Cloud and use it through a web console, a command-line tool or an API. The provider owns the data centers; you decide what to run and how much of it.","The widely used definition from the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) lists five characteristics. On-demand self-service means you can create resources yourself in minutes without asking anyone. Broad network access means you reach them over the network from many kinds of device. Resource pooling means the provider shares large pools of hardware across many customers. Rapid elasticity means capacity can grow and shrink quickly with demand. Measured service means usage is metered, so you pay for what you use.","Digital transformation is a broader idea. It means an organization uses new digital technology to change how it works, how it serves customers and how it creates value. Moving servers to someone else's data center is not transformation by itself. Transformation is when a bank launches a new mobile feature in weeks instead of a year, a retailer personalizes offers from real-time data, or a hospital uses AI to reduce paperwork for nurses.","The cloud drives this change because it removes many of the old limits. Teams no longer wait months for hardware, so they can experiment cheaply and drop ideas that fail. Managed services for data, AI and security give small teams capabilities that used to need large specialist groups. Global infrastructure lets a company reach customers in new countries without building data centers there. Google describes this combination of infrastructure, data, AI and collaboration tools as helping organizations become more agile and data-driven.","For the Cloud Digital Leader exam, keep the business view in mind. Questions are usually framed around an organization's goal, such as speed, cost, innovation or reliability, and ask which cloud idea or product helps. Be ready to explain the difference between simply using cloud technology and actually transforming how the business operates."],"terms":[["Cloud computing","On-demand access to shared computing resources over a network, paid for by use."],["Digital transformation","Using digital technology to change how an organization operates, serves customers and creates value."],["On-demand self-service","Users provision resources themselves, when needed, without human interaction with the provider."],["Measured service","Resource use is metered and reported, which enables pay-as-you-go billing."]],"example":"A regional insurer used to wait three months for new servers before starting any project. After moving to Google Cloud it builds a claims-photo analysis prototype in two weeks using managed storage and a pre-trained image API, shows it to customers, and only then decides to invest further.","tip":"If an answer only changes where servers run but not how the business works, it is migration, not transformation. Look for answers about speed, experimentation, data-driven decisions and new customer value.","check":[["Name the five NIST characteristics of cloud computing.","On-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity and measured service."],["Why is moving servers to the cloud not the same as digital transformation?","Transformation changes how the organization works and creates value; a move with no change to processes, culture or products only changes the location of the servers."]]},{"t":"Business benefits of the cloud: scalability, elasticity, agility, reliability and strategic value","body":["Organizations adopt the cloud for business reasons, and the exam expects you to match each benefit to a situation. Most questions describe a problem, such as a traffic spike, slow projects or outages, and ask which benefit or approach solves it.","Scalability is the ability to handle more work by adding resources. You can scale up (vertically) by moving to a bigger machine, or scale out (horizontally) by adding more machines. Elasticity is scalability that happens automatically and in both directions: resources are added when demand rises and removed when it falls, so you do not pay for idle capacity. A shop with a holiday peak benefits from elasticity; a company growing steadily year after year benefits from scalability.","Agility is the speed at which a business can try new ideas and respond to change. In the cloud a team can create an environment in minutes, test an idea and delete everything if it fails. That lowers the cost of failure and encourages experimentation. Managed services add to agility because teams spend less time on patching, backups and hardware, and more on features customers see.","Reliability means a service keeps working when parts fail. Cloud providers offer many data centers grouped into zones and regions, and managed services that replicate data automatically. Designing across zones and regions lets an application survive failures that would take down a single on-premises server room. Flexibility is related: you can choose from many types of compute, storage and pricing to match each workload.","Strategic value is the bigger picture. By moving undifferentiated work, such as running data centers, to a provider, a company can focus its people and money on what makes it different. It also gains access to capabilities it could not easily build, such as large-scale analytics, AI and global networks. Cost matters too, but the exam often treats cost savings as one benefit among several rather than the main reason."],"terms":[["Scalability","The ability to increase capacity to handle more load, by scaling up or out."],["Elasticity","Automatically adding and removing resources as demand changes, so capacity follows load."],["Agility","The ability to move quickly: provision, experiment and change direction with little delay or cost."],["Reliability","The ability of a service to keep working correctly when components fail."]],"example":"A tax-preparation website is quiet for ten months and extremely busy before the filing deadline. With autoscaling on Google Cloud it runs a few instances most of the year and hundreds in the final weeks, paying for the peak only while it lasts.","tip":"Scalability is about being able to grow; elasticity is growing and shrinking automatically with demand. A question that mentions short spikes followed by quiet periods is pointing at elasticity.","check":[["A startup wants to test a new product cheaply and drop it quickly if it fails. Which benefit is this?","Agility: resources can be created and removed quickly with no up-front purchase."],["What is the difference between scaling up and scaling out?","Scaling up moves to a larger machine; scaling out adds more machines that share the load."]]},{"t":"CapEx vs OpEx and total cost of ownership (TCO) when moving to the cloud","body":["Capital expenditure (CapEx) is money spent up front on long-lived assets, such as servers, storage arrays, network gear and buildings. The asset is recorded on the balance sheet and depreciated over several years. Operating expenditure (OpEx) is ongoing spending on day-to-day costs, such as rent, electricity, salaries and subscriptions, recorded as an expense in the period it is used."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deployment options: on-premises, private cloud, public cloud, hybrid cloud and multicloud","body":["Organizations can run IT in several places, and many use more than one. The exam expects you to recognize each model from a short description and to know why a company might choose it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and serverless, and what the customer manages in each","body":["Cloud services are grouped by how much of the stack the provider manages for you. Think of the stack from the bottom up: facilities, physical hardware, networking, virtualization, operating system, runtime and middleware, application, and finally data and access. The further up the provider manages, the less work you have, and the less control you have."],"locked":true},{"t":"The shared responsibility model and how it changes with the service model","body":["In the cloud, security and operations are shared between the provider and the customer. The shared responsibility model describes who does what. Misunderstanding it is a common cause of incidents: customers sometimes assume the provider protects things that are actually the customer's job."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google Cloud global infrastructure: regions, zones, edge points of presence and Google's private network","body":["Google Cloud runs on the same global infrastructure that serves Google's own products. Understanding its building blocks helps you design for performance, reliability and data location, and it appears in many exam questions."],"locked":true},{"t":"Network performance basics: bandwidth, latency and choosing locations close to users","body":["Two measurements describe most network performance questions. Bandwidth is how much data can be sent per second, like the width of a pipe. Latency is how long it takes a piece of data to travel from one point to another and back, like the length of the pipe. Throughput is the amount of data actually transferred in practice, which depends on both."],"locked":true},{"t":"Open source, open standards and avoiding vendor lock-in","body":["Vendor lock-in happens when moving away from a provider becomes so costly or difficult that you are effectively stuck. It can come from proprietary data formats, APIs that exist only on one platform, skills that do not transfer, or contracts. Leaders worry about lock-in because it weakens their negotiating position and makes it harder to adopt better technology later."],"locked":true},{"t":"Leading digital transformation: culture, skills and change management in a cloud adoption","body":["Technology is usually the easy part of a cloud adoption. The harder part is people: how teams are organized, how decisions are made, which skills people have and how willing they are to work differently. The Cloud Digital Leader exam is aimed at people who help lead this change, so it expects you to understand the human side."],"locked":true},{"t":"Why data matters: using data to drive decisions, products and innovation","body":["Every organization generates data: sales transactions, website clicks, sensor readings, support tickets, documents and more. On its own this data is just a cost to store. It becomes valuable when it is turned into insight that changes a decision, improves a product or creates something new. The Cloud Digital Leader exam treats data as a strategic asset and asks how Google Cloud helps unlock its value."],"locked":true},{"t":"Structured, semi-structured and unstructured data","body":["Data comes in different shapes, and the shape decides where it is best stored and how it is analyzed. The exam expects you to recognize the three main types and match them to Google Cloud services."],"locked":true},{"t":"Databases, data warehouses and data lakes: what each is for","body":["Three kinds of data store appear again and again in the exam. They are not rivals; most organizations use all three for different jobs."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Storage and its storage classes: Standard, Nearline, Coldline and Archive","body":["Cloud Storage is Google Cloud's object storage service. It stores any kind of file, called an object, in containers called buckets. It is designed for very high durability, scales without limits you need to plan for, and is used for website content, backups, data lakes, media, and data for analytics and machine learning. Objects are reached through the console, the `gcloud storage` command, client libraries or HTTPS."],"locked":true},{"t":"Relational databases on Google Cloud: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB and Spanner","body":["Relational databases store data in tables of rows and columns with defined relationships, use SQL, and support transactions that keep data consistent: either all of a change happens or none of it does. They run most business applications, from online shops to banking. Google Cloud offers three main managed relational services, and the exam asks you to choose between them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Non-relational databases on Google Cloud: Firestore and Bigtable","body":["Non-relational, or NoSQL, databases store data in models other than related tables. They trade some relational features, such as complex joins, for flexibility or very large scale. Google Cloud's two main managed NoSQL services on the exam are Firestore and Bigtable, and they solve quite different problems."],"locked":true},{"t":"BigQuery: serverless data warehouse and analytics","body":["BigQuery is Google Cloud's fully managed, serverless data warehouse. You load or stream data into it, or point it at data in other places, and query it with standard SQL. There are no servers, clusters or indexes to manage, and it can scan very large data sets, terabytes and beyond, quickly. It is one of the most important products for the data section of the exam."],"locked":true},{"t":"Streaming and processing data: Pub/Sub, Dataflow and Dataproc","body":["Data rarely arrives ready to analyze. It must be collected from many sources, cleaned, joined and transformed, then delivered to where it is used. This can happen in batches, such as a nightly load of yesterday's sales, or as a stream, where events are processed continuously within seconds. Google Cloud has managed services for both."],"locked":true},{"t":"Business intelligence with Looker and Looker Studio","body":["Business intelligence (BI) is the practice of turning data into reports, dashboards and answers that people across a business can use to make decisions. Data in a warehouse only creates value when someone can see and act on it, so BI is the final step of many data journeys. Google Cloud offers two related tools with different purposes."],"locked":true},{"t":"Moving data to Google Cloud: Database Migration Service, BigQuery Data Transfer Service, Storage Transfer Service and Transfer Appliance","body":["Before data can create value in the cloud, it has to get there. Google Cloud offers several transfer services, and the exam tests whether you can pick the right one from the type of data, its source and the network available."],"locked":true},{"t":"Data governance: quality, security, access control and cataloging","body":["Data governance is the set of policies, roles and tools that make sure data is accurate, secure, discoverable and used appropriately. Without it, organizations collect more data but trust it less: nobody knows which table is correct, sensitive data leaks to the wrong people, and regulators ask questions nobody can answer. Good governance is what makes data safe and useful at the same time."],"locked":true},{"t":"Artificial intelligence, machine learning and generative AI: definitions and differences","body":["These three terms are often used interchangeably, but they are nested ideas, and the exam expects you to tell them apart."],"locked":true},{"t":"Business problems machine learning can solve, and when ML is not the right tool","body":["Machine learning is powerful, but it is a tool for specific kinds of problems. A digital leader should be able to spot where ML adds value and where simpler approaches are better."],"locked":true},{"t":"Data quality for machine learning: accuracy, completeness, representativeness and bias","body":["A machine learning model learns only from the data it is trained on. If that data is poor, the model will be poor, however advanced the algorithm. This is often summarized as 'garbage in, garbage out', and it is why data quality is part of the AI section of the exam."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI: Google's AI Principles, fairness, explainability, privacy and accountability","body":["AI systems can make decisions that affect people's jobs, loans, health and safety, and generative AI can produce content that is wrong or harmful. Responsible AI is the practice of designing, building and using AI in ways that are safe, fair and trustworthy. Google Cloud treats it as part of every AI project, and the exam expects you to know its main ideas."],"locked":true},{"t":"Pre-trained AI APIs: Vision, Natural Language, Speech-to-Text, Text-to-Speech and Translation","body":["The fastest way to add AI to an application is to call a pre-trained model through an API. Google has already trained these models on very large data sets, so you send your data, such as an image, a document or an audio file, and receive results in seconds. You need no training data, no ML expertise and no infrastructure, and you typically pay per use, with a free monthly allowance for many of them."],"locked":true},{"t":"BigQuery ML: building and using models with SQL where the data already lives","body":["Many organizations keep their most valuable business data in BigQuery, and many of their analysts know SQL well but not Python or ML frameworks. BigQuery ML closes that gap: it lets you create, train, evaluate and use machine learning models with SQL statements, directly inside BigQuery."],"locked":true},{"t":"Vertex AI: the unified ML platform, AutoML and custom training","body":["Vertex AI is Google Cloud's unified platform for building, deploying and managing machine learning and generative AI. Before unified platforms, teams stitched together separate tools for preparing data, training, tracking experiments, serving models and monitoring them. Vertex AI brings these steps together, so data scientists and engineers work in one place with consistent security and governance."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing an AI approach: pre-trained API, BigQuery ML, AutoML or custom model","body":["Google Cloud offers several ways to add AI, and the exam frequently asks which one fits a scenario. The choice balances four things: how common or unique the problem is, the data you have, the skills on the team and how quickly you need results."],"locked":true},{"t":"Generative AI on Google Cloud: Gemini models, Vertex AI Model Garden and Vertex AI Studio","body":["Generative AI lets organizations create text, images, code and more from natural language prompts. Google Cloud provides access to Google's own foundation models and to many others, along with tools to test, customize and deploy them securely for business use."],"locked":true},{"t":"Grounding, agents and AI-powered search and conversation for business","body":["Foundation models are trained on huge amounts of public data up to a point in time. On their own, they do not know a company's internal policies, product catalog or yesterday's prices, and when they lack information they may produce fluent but incorrect answers, called hallucinations. Business uses of generative AI therefore depend on connecting models to the right data and actions."],"locked":true},{"t":"AI infrastructure: GPUs and Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)","body":["Training and running modern machine learning models, especially large deep learning and generative models, requires enormous amounts of computation, mostly large matrix and vector math. General-purpose CPUs can do this work, but slowly. Specialized hardware called accelerators speeds it up dramatically, and access to accelerators at scale is one reason organizations use the cloud for AI."],"locked":true},{"t":"Why modernize: benefits of moving infrastructure and applications to the cloud","body":["Many organizations run applications on infrastructure that is expensive to maintain, slow to change and hard to scale: aging servers, software nearing end of support, and manual processes. Modernization means updating infrastructure and applications so they can take advantage of the cloud. It is a central theme of this exam section."],"locked":true},{"t":"Migration approaches: retire, retain, rehost (lift and shift), replatform, refactor and reimagine","body":["When organizations plan a cloud migration, they choose an approach for each application. The industry often describes these as the 'Rs'. Google Cloud's training uses terms such as lift and shift, move and improve, and refactor or rebuild; the exam expects you to match each approach to a situation and understand the trade-off between speed and benefit."],"locked":true},{"t":"Virtual machines with Compute Engine: machine types, managed instance groups and autoscaling","body":["Compute Engine is Google Cloud's infrastructure as a service (IaaS) offering for virtual machines (VMs). A VM behaves like a physical server: you choose an operating system image (Linux or Windows), a machine type and disks, and you have full control over the software installed. Compute Engine is the usual landing place for lift-and-shift migrations and for workloads that need a specific OS, kernel settings or licensed software."],"locked":true},{"t":"Keeping existing platforms: Google Cloud VMware Engine and Bare Metal Solution","body":["Some workloads cannot easily be moved to standard cloud VMs or rewritten. They may depend on a particular virtualization platform, specialized hardware, or licensing terms tied to physical servers. Google Cloud offers two services that let organizations move these workloads to the cloud while keeping their existing platform."],"locked":true},{"t":"Containers: what they are and why they make applications portable","body":["A container is a lightweight, standalone package that includes an application's code together with everything it needs to run: the runtime, libraries, system tools and settings. Containers solve a long-standing problem in software, summed up as 'it works on my machine'. Because the container carries its dependencies, the application behaves the same on a developer's laptop, in a test environment, in a data center or in any cloud."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE): managed Kubernetes in Standard and Autopilot modes","body":["Kubernetes is an open source platform for orchestrating containers: it decides where containers run, keeps the desired number running, restarts failed ones, scales them, and connects them with networking and load balancing. It began as a Google project based on Google's experience running containers internally, and it is now the industry standard, available in every major cloud and on-premises."],"locked":true},{"t":"Serverless computing: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","body":["Serverless computing lets developers run code without provisioning or managing servers. The platform handles infrastructure, operating system patching, scaling and availability. Applications scale automatically with demand, often down to zero when idle, and you pay for the resources used while handling requests or events. Servers still exist, but they are entirely the provider's concern. Serverless lets small teams deliver quickly and avoids paying for idle capacity."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing compute for a workload: VMs vs containers vs serverless","body":["Google Cloud gives you a spectrum of compute options. At one end, you control everything and manage everything; at the other, you manage almost nothing but accept the platform's conventions. The exam frequently gives a workload description and asks which option fits, so it helps to have a clear decision process."],"locked":true},{"t":"Monoliths vs microservices and application modernization","body":["A monolithic application is built and deployed as a single unit. All its features, such as user accounts, catalog, cart, payments and notifications, live in one codebase and run as one process or package. Monoliths are simple to start with, but as they grow they become hard to change: a small update requires testing and redeploying the whole application, one bug or overloaded feature can bring everything down, and scaling means copying the entire application even if only one part is busy. Large teams working in one codebase also slow one another down."],"locked":true},{"t":"APIs and API management with Apigee","body":["An application programming interface (API) is a defined way for one piece of software to request data or actions from another. When a mobile banking app shows your balance, it calls the bank's API; when an online shop shows shipping rates, it calls a carrier's API. APIs hide the complexity of the systems behind them and let those systems be reused in new ways."],"locked":true},{"t":"Hybrid and multicloud with GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos)","body":["Many organizations run applications across several environments: their own data centers, Google Cloud, other public clouds and edge locations such as stores or factories. They may do so for regulation, latency, existing investments, mergers or to avoid dependence on one provider. The challenge is that each environment has its own tools, security settings and operating processes, so teams spend time on inconsistency instead of value."],"locked":true},{"t":"Core security concepts: confidentiality, integrity, availability, privacy, control and compliance","body":["Security discussions use a small set of core terms, and the Cloud Digital Leader exam expects you to recognize them in business scenarios. Three of them form the classic CIA triad."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud security vs on-premises security, and shared responsibility for security","body":["In a traditional on-premises environment, the organization is responsible for every layer of security: guarding the building, securing the hardware and its supply chain, patching firmware and operating systems, protecting the network, managing identities and protecting the data. Doing all of this well requires large budgets and specialist staff that many organizations do not have."],"locked":true},{"t":"Common cloud threats: misconfiguration, compromised credentials, phishing, malware and ransomware","body":["Knowing the common threats helps leaders invest in the right controls. Many cloud incidents are not caused by sophisticated attacks on the provider but by weaknesses on the customer's side."],"locked":true},{"t":"Zero trust and defense in depth","body":["Traditional security used a castle-and-moat model: a strong perimeter firewall protected the corporate network, and anything inside was trusted. That model fits poorly today. People work from home and on the move, applications run in the cloud and are reached over the internet, and an attacker who gets inside the perimeter, for example through a phished laptop, can move around freely."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google's secure infrastructure: data centers, custom hardware, Titan chips and the private network","body":["Google Cloud runs on the same infrastructure that Google uses for its own services, which serve billions of users. Google designs security into every layer of this infrastructure, from the physical buildings up to the services, and publishes an overview of the design. The exam expects you to know the main layers at a high level."],"locked":true},{"t":"Encryption at rest and in transit, and key management with Cloud KMS","body":["Encryption transforms readable data into ciphertext that can only be read with the right key. It protects confidentiality even if storage media or network traffic are accessed by someone unauthorized. Google Cloud applies encryption by default in two states."],"locked":true},{"t":"Identity and access management: principals, roles, least privilege and two-step verification","body":["Identity and access management (IAM) answers the question: who can do what on which resource? In the cloud, where resources are reached over the internet, IAM is one of the most important security controls, and misused access is behind many incidents."],"locked":true},{"t":"Network and perimeter security: firewall rules, Cloud Armor and VPC Service Controls","body":["Even in a zero trust world, network controls remain an important layer of defense in depth. Google Cloud provides several, each protecting against different threats."],"locked":true},{"t":"Security operations: Security Command Center, audit logs and Google Security Operations","body":["Security operations (SecOps) is the continuous work of detecting, investigating and responding to threats. Preventive controls are never perfect, so organizations need visibility into what is happening and the ability to react quickly. Google Cloud provides tools at several levels."],"locked":true},{"t":"Data residency, data sovereignty and Assured Workloads","body":["Many organizations, especially in the public sector, healthcare and finance, must meet rules about where data is stored and who can access it. These rules come from national laws, industry regulations and customer contracts, and they are a common concern when moving to the cloud."],"locked":true},{"t":"Compliance and transparency: compliance reports, Access Transparency and Google's trust principles","body":["Moving sensitive workloads to the cloud requires trust in the provider. Organizations need evidence that the provider meets recognized standards, clear commitments about how their data is handled, and visibility into any access by the provider's staff."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud financial governance and FinOps: shared accountability for cloud cost","body":["In a traditional data center, spending is controlled up front: finance approves a hardware purchase, and afterwards the cost is fixed. In the cloud, any engineer with permission can create resources in minutes, and costs accumulate by the second. That flexibility is valuable, but without new practices it can lead to surprise bills, idle resources and no clear idea of who spent what."],"locked":true},{"t":"The Google Cloud resource hierarchy: organization, folders, projects and resources","body":["Google Cloud organizes everything a company uses into a hierarchy. The hierarchy is the foundation for managing access, applying policies and tracking costs, so designing it well is one of the first steps in a cloud adoption."],"locked":true},{"t":"Policy inheritance: IAM allow policies and organization policies through the hierarchy","body":["One of the main benefits of the resource hierarchy is that policies are inherited. A policy set on a node applies to that node and everything beneath it. This lets administrators manage access and guardrails for thousands of resources centrally, instead of configuring each one."],"locked":true},{"t":"Controlling costs: billing accounts, budgets and alerts, quotas, labels and billing export","body":["Google Cloud provides several tools to see, allocate and control spending. The exam often tests what each one does and, just as importantly, what it does not do."],"locked":true},{"t":"Pricing models and discounts: pay-as-you-go, sustained use discounts, committed use discounts and Spot VMs","body":["Google Cloud's default pricing is pay-as-you-go: you pay for the resources you use, with no up-front commitment, and most compute is billed per second. This suits new, variable or unpredictable workloads. Google Cloud also offers ways to pay less when your usage has particular patterns, and the exam expects you to match each to a scenario."],"locked":true},{"t":"DevOps and Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) principles","body":["Traditionally, developers wanted to release new features quickly, while operations teams wanted stability and resisted change. The result was slow releases, friction and blame. DevOps and Site Reliability Engineering are approaches that resolve this tension, and the exam expects you to know their main ideas."],"locked":true},{"t":"SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets","body":["Reliability needs to be measured and agreed, not just hoped for. Site Reliability Engineering uses a small set of terms that the exam tests directly. They build on one another."],"locked":true},{"t":"Reliability and disaster recovery: redundancy across zones and regions, backups, RTO and RPO","body":["Failures are inevitable: disks fail, software has bugs, people make mistakes and occasionally a whole data center or region has problems. Reliable systems are designed to keep working, or to recover quickly, when things go wrong. The cloud makes many reliability techniques easier and cheaper, but they still must be chosen and configured."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google Cloud Observability: Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging, Cloud Trace and Error Reporting","body":["You cannot run a reliable service without knowing how it behaves. Observability is the ability to understand the internal state of a system from the data it produces, mainly metrics, logs and traces. Google Cloud Observability (formerly known as Stackdriver and then Cloud Operations suite) is a set of integrated services for this. Much of the data is collected automatically from Google Cloud services, and agents or libraries collect more from VMs and applications."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google Cloud Customer Care: support plans and when to use them","body":["Even well-run cloud environments sometimes need help from the provider: a service behaving unexpectedly, a quota that must be raised quickly, a billing question or advice on architecture. Google Cloud Customer Care offers several levels of support, and organizations choose one based on how critical their workloads are."],"locked":true},{"t":"Sustainability: Google's carbon-free energy goals, low-carbon regions and the Carbon Footprint tool","body":["Data centers use large amounts of electricity, so IT has a real environmental impact. Many organizations now have sustainability targets and must report their emissions, including those from cloud services. Moving to an efficient cloud and choosing low-carbon options can reduce a company's IT footprint, and the exam includes sustainability as part of operating in the cloud."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-09-25"});
